    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Upanishads are the latest layer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and turn from ritual toward questions of the self (Atman) and ultimate reality (Brahman).</li><li>They hold a plurality of ideas: some are described as monistic, others, such as the Katha, as dualistic, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> schools read them in divergent ways.</li><li>Scholars give only broad dates, from about 800 to 300 BCE for the principal texts, and new texts called Upanishads went on being composed into the early modern era.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1656 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shukoh</a> produced the Sirr-i-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, a Persian translation of 50 Upanishads; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version made from a Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was published in 1801 and 1802, and Schopenhauer kept a copy by his side.</p></aside>
    </section>

